    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Ford Rolls Out the Ranger MS-RT Champion Edition: You Can Choose Any Color As Long as It Is Black\nThree Failed Brake Pedal Brackets Put Chinese Luxury Minivan Under Investigation\n22 photos\nPhoto: Dongchedi\nThree units of the Maextro V800 went through emergency stop testing at the beginning of October 2026. All three Chinese luxury minivans suffered brake pedal bracket failures, which is unacceptable in this day and age, regardless of vehicle segment and body style. For a luxury MPV that launched barely two months earlier, that is a very serious problem.\nChina 's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology opened an investigation on October 15, 2026, and this investigation could be a lot more interesting than Maextro's rebuttal of the failed tests. It all started on October 8 with Dongchedi, namely the ByteDance-controlled automotive platform that is also known as DCar.\nThe video published that day saw Dongchedi perform emergency braking tests from 100 kilometers per hour (62 miles per hour) to a standstill on a closed course. The first Maextro V800's brake pedal bracket failed on its third attempt, the second on its fourth, and a subsequently purchased Maextro V800 gave up the ghost on attempt two.\nNeedless to say, there's a distinction to make between a failed bracket and a wholesale braking system defect. According to Dongchedi, the fractures prevented normal brake pedal operation. The emergency braking function did activate, though, providing deceleration after the brake pedal bracket had failed.\nDue to said problem, stopping distances increased from around 38 meters (125 feet) on average to approximately 140 meters (459 feet) when the emergency braking function took over. Dongchedi gave a few suggestions about bracket geometry, reinforcement, and material, but all of these details have yet to be professionally investigated by actual safety boffins and Maextro engineers.\nPhoto: Dongchedi Speaking of, Maextro answered on the evening of October 8, 2026. Describing itself as an ultra-luxury electric vehicle brand, the brand in question should be viewed as a joint venture between the JAC Group and Huawei. Per the automaker's representatives, no bracket fractures had been reported by V800 customers since deliveries began. Maextro also maintained that development and validation exceeded industry requirements.\nThe fun part? Maextro said the Dongchedi video shows nonstandard extreme conditions. Truth be told, nonstandard extreme braking conditions can also occur during daily driving scenarios. While Maextro stopped short of describing the Dongchedi test as invalid, the absence of customer failure reports does not explain the three Dongchedi failures. Whataboutism, if you will.\nWorse still for Maextro , the company also promised engineering changes before the MIIT started looking into this matter on October 15, 2026. The automaker's October 8 response pledged an improved bracket base design, a free upgrade for existing Maextro V800 owners, improved testing parameters, and changes to production quality management.\nA promised free upgrade is not the same as a recall, though, and that is probably the reason why the MIIT started an investigation of its own. Be that as it may, the October 15 notice is much narrower than the argument around it. The MIIT investigates the alleged defect with the help of the Cyberspace Administration and the State Administration for Market Regulation.\nVia // MIIT\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nMaextro V800 Maextro braking test China MPV\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Certain 2026 Ram 2500 Trucks Could Lose Stability Control Without Warning",
